  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Bring-Up 7: Retrain Vref for the real board, not the bench"
            prompt="Every training step so far was done on the bench, at essentially full swing, and the memory test passed cleanly there. Installed on the real board - the one Bring-Up 2 measured earlier in this plan - the memory test now fails almost every word. CS/CA, write levelling, and per-bit deskew are all still correctly trained. What changed?"
            hint="Compare the real DQ swing Bring-Up 2 measured on this board against the Vref this chip is still using. Was Vref retrained for this board's actual channel, or carried over from the bench?"
            explanation="Vref is still 0.82 - left over from training it on the bench's essentially full-swing connection - but this board's real trace only delivers about 30% of that swing (the same number Bring-Up 2 measured). A Vref trained for near-ideal swing sits far outside the real, attenuated eye, so almost every bit slices to the same fixed value no matter what's actually driven. Every other training step (CS/CA timing, write levelling, per-bit deskew) is purely about *when* this chip samples, so none of it cared about the bench-vs-board swing difference - Vref is the one setting that does, and it's exactly why real bring-up retrains Vref specifically against the real, installed channel rather than carrying over a bench value. Retrain Vref back toward the real channel's actual centre and the test passes cleanly again - the same real lesson as DDR Homework 2 in the flat set."/>
</LABCHAIN>
